It is completely understandable why you might feel skeptical. There is a massive difference between the "Hollywood" version of autism—think _Rain Man_ or _The Good Doctor_—and the actual clinical reality. When your sister suggests you might be autistic, she likely sees certain traits that "look" like her perception of it, but clinicians use a much more specific, evidence-based framework.

In the UK, clinicians typically use the ICD-11 (or the DSM-5 in private or international contexts) to guide a diagnosis. They generally look for a pattern of "persistent deficits" or differences in two main areas.

