FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Outsourcing Writing to AI Bypasses the Cognitive Strain That Builds Professional Competence.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T18:10:06+00:00
tags: [ai, cognitive-fitness, professional-development, writing]
title: Outsourcing Writing to AI Bypasses the Cognitive Strain That Builds Professional Competence
---
CONTENT_START:

## Outsourcing Writing to AI Bypasses the Cognitive Strain That Builds Professional Competence

Do not use AI to draft emails, reports, or memos. The cognitive strain of writing is not a cost to be optimised away—it is the mechanism through which one develops a deep understanding of one's own domain. Removing that strain produces fluent-sounding "work slop" while atrophying the business understanding that writing was building.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Commitment, Consistency, and Sunk Cost Fallacy.md
---
aliases: ["Sunk Cost Fallacy"]
created: 2025-08-23T17:07:39Z
creation_date: "2025-08-23"
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:35+00:00
status: ""
tags: ["TheHuman/Cognition/bias", "TheHuman/Psychology"]
title: Commitment, Consistency, and Sunk Cost Fallacy
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Once people commit to a position or a course of action, even in a small way, they feel internal and external pressure to behave consistently with that commitment. The more effort, time, or emotion they invest (sunk costs), the more they will rationalise its importance to justify the investment.

In an argument, for example, individuals may refuse to back down not because of the issue's importance, but because they have invested their ego and must now see it through, manufacturing reasons why it is a vital matter of principle. This reinforces the bubble of [[Contextual Myopia and Goal Displacement]].

---
