FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Cognitive Offloading Frees Mental Resources for Decision-Making and Problem-Solving.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [cognition, mental-models, pkm, productivity]
title: Cognitive Offloading Frees Mental Resources for Decision-Making and Problem-Solving
---
CONTENT_START:

## Cognitive Offloading Frees Mental Resources for Decision-Making and Problem-Solving

Offloading thoughts onto an external medium—paper, screen, or a structured note system—frees limited short-term mental resources that would otherwise be spent on retention. The brain's short-term capacity is bounded (approximately seven items in a seven-second window), meaning any information held internally competes directly with the capacity needed for reasoning, deciding, and problem-solving. Once externalised, the mind no longer needs to hold the content—only the knowledge that it has been captured.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Unrealistic expectations are the main obstacle to building ADHD routines.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-10-29T16:40:00Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:24+00:00
source: "[[MOC - ADHD Routine Building Strategies]]"
status: "seedling"
tags: ["TheHuman/Habits", "TheHuman/Health/ADHD", "TheHuman/Mindset", "topic/productivity"]
title: Unrealistic expectations are the main obstacle to building ADHD routines
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The primary obstacle to building sustainable routines for individuals with ADHD is not inconsistency, but setting unrealistic expectations. Trying to change too much too quickly leads to overwhelm and failure, reinforcing a negative cycle. The key is to start with small, manageable changes.

This principle is sometimes called "one degree of change"—making incremental adjustments rather than attempting dramatic overnight transformations. When we push too far beyond our current capacity, we create unsustainable stress that leads to abandonment of the new behavior. Sustainable change happens at the boundary of the proximal zone of development, where growth feels challenging but achievable, not overwhelming.
---
