FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Self-Observation is the Foundation of Routine Building.md
---
aliases: ["Know Yourself to Build Your Routine"]
created: 2025-10-29T16:10:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:27+00:00
status: "seedling"
tags: ["energy-management", "routine", "self-awareness", "sensory-processing"]
title: Self-Observation is the Foundation of Routine Building
type: "strategy"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The foundation of any effective and sustainable routine is deep self-knowledge. Before implementing a new structure, one must first engage in a period of self-observation to understand their unique needs, patterns, and sensitivities.

This involves tracking daily activities, moods, and energy levels to identify peak performance times and periods of likely overwhelm. It is also critical to note sensory sensitivities—such as reactions to light, sound, or textures—as these environmental factors can significantly impact one's ability to follow a routine. A week of observation can reveal crucial patterns that allow a routine to be tailored to the individual, rather than based on generic advice.
---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Bash - Error Handling.md
---
created: 2026-02-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-02-15T15:53:50+00:00
tags: [bash, devops, reference, shell]
title: Bash - Error Handling
---
---

### The Dangerous Default

Bash does NOT stop on failure by default. If a command fails, bash just moves to the next line. This is unlike most programming languages and is the root cause of most bash footguns.

```sh
#!/bin/bash
rm important-lockfile.txt    # fails silently if file doesn't exist
deploy_application            # runs anyway — potentially dangerous
```

---
---
