FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Bessie's Working Memory is Overwhelmed by Large Information Volumes.md
---
aliases: ["Bessie's Working Memory Challenges"]
created: 2025-07-18T00:00:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
see_also: []
status: ""
superseded_by: ""
supersedes: ""
tags: []
title: "Bessie's Working Memory is Overwhelmed by Large Information Volumes"
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Bessie's diagnoses of ADHD and dyslexia present significant challenges with executive functions, particularly working memory. This manifests as difficulty concentrating, forgetfulness, and struggling with organization.

A specific, practical example of this is her difficulty copying large amounts of text from a classroom board. She struggles to hold the information in her mind long enough to transcribe it, leading to a slow and frustrating process of constantly looking back and forth. This demonstrates that her working memory can be easily overwhelmed by a high volume of information, a key consideration for her learning needs.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Flexible routines accommodate hyperfocus and task-switching patterns.md
---
aliases: ["Accommodates ADHD patterns", "Flexible for hyperfocus"]
created: 2025-10-30T17:37:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:33+00:00
source: "[[MOC - How To Create And Use Mini Routines]]"
status: "seedling"
tags: ["flexibility", "hyperfocus", "task-switching", "TheHuman/Health/ADHD"]
title: Flexible routines accommodate hyperfocus and task-switching patterns
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Built-in flexibility within event-based routine structures naturally accommodates common ADHD behavioral patterns including task-switching impulses, extended hyperfocus episodes, and dopamine-seeking behavior, without requiring these patterns to be suppressed or creating guilt when they occur.

Rigid time-based schedules often conflict with ADHD neurology: a hyperfocus session on an interesting problem may extend well beyond its allocated time slot, derailing subsequent activities and creating shame. Event-based structures eliminate this conflict—the hyperfocus session completes, then the next anchored activity begins, preserving the routine sequence without forcing premature disengagement.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Set Theory Requires Distinct Objects.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-08-30T09:00:44Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01T00:00:00.000Z"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:27+00:00
status: "seedling"
tags: ["foundations", "set-theory", "topic/maths"]
title: Set Theory Requires Distinct Objects
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The idea that distinction is necessary for numbers is not just philosophical; it is embedded in the formal foundations of modern mathematics.

Set theory, a foundational system for mathematics, is based on the concept of a set as a collection of _distinct_ objects. The very first axiom assumes that we have objects that can be told apart from one another.

---
