FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Distorted Negative Self-Image.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-06-22T17:43:30Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
see_also: []
status: ""
superseded_by: ""
supersedes: ""
tags: ["TheHuman/Health/ADHD"]
title: Distorted Negative Self-Image
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

For individuals with ADHD, an inability to accurately observe their own performance, coupled with heightened sensitivity to perceived criticism and a history of underachievement, often leads to a "distorted negative self-image".

Without consistent, clear feedback, Bessie is left to interpret her performance, which can be misread and contribute to feelings of shame and low self-regard, further impacting her willingness to engage with opportunities. The _ADHD 2.0_ authors state that "shame is the most disabling learning disability".

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/La Fontaines Fables Served as Courtly Education in 17th Century France.md
---
aliases: ["La Fontaine's Fables"]
created: 2025-11-01T21:37:00Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:32+00:00
status: "evergreen"
tags: ["fable", "french-literature", "history", "la-fontaine", "storytelling"]
title: La Fontaines Fables Served as Courtly Education in 17th Century France
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

In the 17th century, Jean de La Fontaine adapted the classic fable form for the French court, creating sophisticated and witty poems that served as a tool for courtly education.

La Fontaine's fables were not just for children; they were complex works of social and political commentary aimed at an adult audience, including the aristocracy and King Louis XIV himself. They used the traditional animal allegory to subtly critique human behavior, social hierarchies, and political power.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/The Versailles Labyrinth Used Fable Installations for Visual Storytelling.md
---
aliases: ["Versailles Fable Labyrinth"]
created: 2025-11-01T21:38:00Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:24+00:00
status: "evergreen"
tags: ["art", "fable", "history", "SoftwareEngineering/Architecture", "storytelling", "versailles"]
title: The Versailles Labyrinth Used Fable Installations for Visual Storytelling
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The Labyrinth of Versailles, a garden maze built for King Louis XIV, represented a shift in fable storytelling towards a visual and interactive medium.

The labyrinth featured 39 fountains, each depicting a different fable, primarily from Aesop and La Fontaine. Visitors would wander through the maze and encounter these sculptural installations, each telling a moral story through its design and an accompanying plaque.

---
