FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/LLMs Exhibit Divergent Strengths Across Development Lifecycle Phases.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [claude, gpt, model-selection, software-engineering, specialization]
title: LLMs Exhibit Divergent Strengths Across Development Lifecycle Phases
---
CONTENT_START:

## LLMs Exhibit Divergent Strengths Across Development Lifecycle Phases

Different LLMs exhibit measurably divergent strengths across development lifecycle phases due to differences in training data and RLHF optimisation targets. Models with stronger creative/planning capabilities (e.g. Claude) perform better in open-ended design and architecture phases; models with stronger execution and consistency (e.g. GPT) perform better in rigid implementation, audit, and structured-output phases. Forcing a single model to handle all phases introduces friction where that model's capabilities are weakest.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Timeboxing Combats the Principle of Least Resistance.md
---
aliases: ["Structure vs Least Resistance"]
created: 2025-11-01T21:02:00Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:24+00:00
status: "evergreen"
tags: ["deep-work", "TheHuman/Psychology", "time-management", "topic/productivity"]
title: Timeboxing Combats the Principle of Least Resistance
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Without a clear structure, it is natural to gravitate toward shallow, easy tasks—the path of least resistance. Timeboxing provides the necessary structure to counteract this tendency.

By scheduling specific blocks for deep, difficult work, an individual creates a firm obligation to tackle these challenging tasks. The pre-defined schedule overrides the default behavior of choosing what is easy in the moment.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/The Totient is the Length of the Exponentiation Cycle.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-08-19T23:32:29Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:24+00:00
status: ""
tags: ["cryptography"]
title: The Totient is the Length of the Exponentiation Cycle
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Within a modular system, raising a number to successive powers generates a sequence of results that eventually repeats. This repeating sequence is the exponentiation cycle.

The totient (λ(n)) is the exact length of this cycle. Its value can only be calculated by knowing the secret [[Prime Numbers Are the Atomic Elements of All Numbers|prime factors]] p and q.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Timeboxing Reduces Decision Fatigue.md
---
aliases: ["Reduced Decision Fatigue"]
created: 2025-11-01T21:04:00Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:24+00:00
status: "evergreen"
tags: ["decision-fatigue", "TheHuman/Psychology", "time-management", "topic/productivity"]
title: Timeboxing Reduces Decision Fatigue
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

By pre-deciding what to work on and when, timeboxing eliminates the constant, low-level question of "What should I do now?"

This conservation of mental energy is a significant benefit. Every decision, no matter how small, consumes cognitive resources. An unstructured day requires repeated decisions about which task to tackle next, leading to [[Decision Fatigue|decision fatigue]].

---
