FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Aesop's Fables Originated in Oral Tradition.md
---
aliases: ["Aesop's Oral Fables"]
created: 2025-11-01T21:35:00Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:37+00:00
status: "evergreen"
tags: ["aesop", "fable", "history", "oral-tradition", "storytelling"]
title: "Aesop's Fables Originated in Oral Tradition"
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The earliest known Western fables are attributed to Aesop, a storyteller believed to have lived in ancient Greece around the 6th century BCE.

These stories were not originally written down but were part of a rich oral tradition. They were short, allegorical tales, typically featuring animals with human-like qualities, that conveyed a moral lesson. Their transmission was primarily through spoken word, making them an accessible form of moral and social instruction for a largely illiterate population.
---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Humiliation.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-05-26T13:51:06Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:33+00:00
status: "null"
tags: ["emotion", "TheHuman/Health/ADHD"]
title: Humiliation
type: "permanent"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Humiliation involves the abasement of pride and a lowering of one's social status, often publicly. It is an emotion typically inflicted by others, where an individual is made to feel debased, ridiculed, or their dignity is stripped away.

While it can involve feelings of shame and embarrassment, the core of humiliation is the experience of being unjustly brought low or having one's standing diminished in the eyes of others, or by an external force. The focus is often on the perceived unfairness or injustice of the situation.

---
