FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Words are Imperfect Representations of Meaning.md
---
aliases: ["Word-as-Shadow Metaphor"]
created: 2025-07-16T17:30:04Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:23+00:00
status: ""
tags: ["language", "metaphor", "paraphrasing", "topic/philosophy"]
title: Words are Imperfect Representations of Meaning
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

A powerful metaphor for the relationship between language and meaning is that words are merely shadows of the true meaning they attempt to convey. A shadow is an incomplete, two-dimensional projection of a three-dimensional object, dependent on the angle of the light. Similarly, words are imperfect approximations of a rich, multi-faceted concept.

Following this metaphor, [[Paraphrasing is a Complex Cognitive Skill|paraphrasing]] is the art of moving the "light source" (one's linguistic and conceptual understanding) to cast a new and different shadow of the same object. We don't change the core meaning, but we illuminate it from a different angle, revealing new shapes and facets that may have been hidden in the original phrasing.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/The Neuroenergetic Deficit Theory Posits Chronic Energy Insufficiency in ADHD Brains.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-10-29T16:38:00Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:25+00:00
source: "[[MOC - The Phases of Task Execution and the Impact of ADHD]]"
status: "seedling"
tags: ["TheHuman/Health/ADHD", "TheHuman/Neuroscience", "theory"]
title: The Neuroenergetic Deficit Theory Posits Chronic Energy Insufficiency in ADHD Brains
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The neuroenergetic deficit theory of ADHD proposes that individuals with the condition can only access 75-85% of the neurocognitive energy of their neurotypical peers. This is due to catecholamine hypofunctionality leading to decreased neuronal energy through impaired astrocyte glucose and glycogen metabolism.

This chronic insufficiency of neural resources particularly affects task initiation, which requires maximal prefrontal cortex activation, and helps explain why all phases of task execution are impacted.
---
