FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Effective Theory.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-08-29T15:17:35Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:34+00:00
status: ""
tags: ["physics", "science", "TheHuman/Cognition/mental-model"]
title: Effective Theory
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

An effective theory is a model of the world that is highly accurate and predictive within a specific, limited domain, even if its foundational principles are known to be incomplete or incorrect on a deeper level.

[[Newtonian Physics as an Effective Theory]] is the classic example; it works for everyday scales but is superseded by General Relativity.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/The Day Can Be Divided Into Three Phases for Habit Mapping.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-10-30T12:05:06Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:25+00:00
source: "[[MOC - The Science of Making and Breaking Habits]]"
status: "seedling"
tags: ["chronobiology", "energy-management", "TheHuman/Habits", "topic/productivity"]
title: The Day Can Be Divided Into Three Phases for Habit Mapping
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The day can be divided into three phases based on natural neurochemical fluctuations, and habits can be mapped to the phase where they are most likely to succeed:

- Phase 1 (0-8 hours after waking): High dopamine and cortisol. Best for high-friction, energy-expensive habits.
- Phase 2 (9-14 hours after waking): Lower dopamine, rising serotonin. Best for less effortful habits, journaling, and reflection.
---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Reframing Negative Thought Patterns in ADHD.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-10-24T15:08:00Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:28+00:00
status: "null"
tags: ["cognitive-behavioral-therapy", "rumination", "self-talk", "TheHuman/Health/ADHD", "TheHuman/Mindset"]
title: Reframing Negative Thought Patterns in ADHD
type: "permanent"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Individuals with Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) often experience a tendency to brood, ruminate, or get stuck in cycles of negative, self-critical thoughts. This can be linked to the brain's "Default Mode Network" (DMN), which, when overactive or dysregulated, can lead to persistent gloomy or self-blaming thought patterns.

Reframing these negative thought patterns is crucial for managing ADHD and improving mental well-being. The solution involves actively engaging the mind in constructive tasks to effectively "outsmart the Demon" of rumination. This means consciously shifting focus and energy towards activities that stimulate the brain's executive functions and reward pathways, thereby diverting the DMN from its negative loops.

---
