source: "[[MOC - Authority Dynamics and ADHD]]"
status: "seedling"
tags: ["coping-strategies", "TheHuman/Health/ADHD", "TheHuman/Psychology"]
title: Defensive Hypervigilance
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Defensive hypervigilance is a coping mechanism where individuals anxiously scan for evidence of their own failures before others can point them out. This self-protective mechanism attempts to guard against unexpected criticism and connects directly to rejection sensitivity. In people with ADHD, this often manifests as constantly looking for mistakes in their own work and feeling intense anxiety about potential judgment.
---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Agentic Autonomy Accelerates Fastest in Domains Where Success Is Verifiable.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [ai-progress, coding, reinforcement-learning, verification]
title: Agentic Autonomy Accelerates Fastest in Domains Where Success Is Verifiable
---
CONTENT_START:

## Agentic Autonomy Accelerates Fastest in Domains Where Success Is Verifiable

AI self-improvement and agentic autonomy accelerate most rapidly in "verifiable domains"—fields like software engineering and model training where success can be mathematically or logically measured without human judgement. Verifiability enables recursive self-improvement: the agent can run experiments, observe outcomes, and update behaviour without requiring a human to evaluate each result.

