FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Evaluation Pipelines Should Distinguish LLM Judges from Deterministic Scripts.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [automation, evaluation, llm-judge, system-design]
title: Evaluation Pipelines Should Distinguish LLM Judges from Deterministic Scripts
---
CONTENT_START:

## Evaluation Pipelines Should Distinguish LLM Judges from Deterministic Scripts

Evaluation pipelines must select their evaluation mechanism based on the nature of the task: subjective or creative tasks require an LLM judge capable of nuanced interpretation, while objective tasks—those with a ground truth or a binary pass/fail—are better handled by a deterministic script. Using an LLM to evaluate an objectively measurable outcome wastes tokens, introduces non-determinism, and creates a risk of the LLM rationalising rather than measuring.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Evil Arises from Malice or Thoughtless Banality — Motivation Is Not Determinative.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [ethics, hannah-arendt, motivation, psychology]
title: Evil Arises from Malice or Thoughtless Banality — Motivation Is Not Determinative
---
CONTENT_START:

## Evil Arises from Malice or Thoughtless Banality—Motivation Is Not Determinative

Evil can arise from two structurally distinct motivational sources: deliberate malicious intent (the Kantian "perverse will"—choosing wrong for its own sake) or "thoughtlessness"—the bureaucratic banality in which immense harm is produced by a perpetrator entirely absorbed in executing a role without engaging moral reflection. Hannah Arendt's analysis of Eichmann demonstrated the second pathway. Both are sufficient for evil; neither is required.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Social Media Now Requires a Sobriety Approach Rather Than a Craftsman Approach.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [addiction, digital-minimalism, dopamine, social-media]
title: Social Media Now Requires a Sobriety Approach Rather Than a Craftsman Approach
---
CONTENT_START:

## Social Media Now Requires a Sobriety Approach Rather Than a Craftsman Approach

Modern algorithmic social media (e.g. TikTok, Instagram Reels) has shifted from a communication utility to an engineered dopamine delivery mechanism. Newport's earlier "craftsman" approach—evaluate pros and cons of each platform, keep those with net value—is no longer sufficient because the platforms' reward engineering makes moderate, intentional use neurologically unstable. The appropriate model is now "sobriety": hard access restrictions that starve the short-term dopamine circuits rather than trying to manage them.

---
