FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Deep Focus on a Single Concept Sparks Innovation.md
---
aliases: ["Focused inquiry generates new ideas"]
created: 2025-07-18T00:00:00Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:35+00:00
status: "null"
tags: ["constraints", "focus", "TheHuman/Cognition/thinking", "TheHuman/Creativity"]
title: Deep Focus on a Single Concept Sparks Innovation
type: "null"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Focusing intently on a small, specific part of a larger topic can unlock novel ideas and insights. This process of "going deep" allows the mind to move beyond a surface-level understanding and discover new connections and possibilities that are missed during a broad, superficial examination.

This deep inquiry is a powerful method for building and refining the complex mental frameworks described in. It is a core component of the [[Thinking & Creating Skills (Your Clever Brain)]] and serves as a potent strategy for [[Problem-Solving Strategies and Heuristics of Expert Developers|expert-level problem-solving]].
---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Long-Term Depression is a Mechanism for Breaking Habits.md
---
aliases: ["LTD"]
created: 2025-10-30T12:05:07Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:31+00:00
source: "[[MOC - The Science of Making and Breaking Habits]]"
status: "seedling"
tags: ["learning", "TheHuman/Habits", "TheHuman/Neuroscience"]
title: Long-Term Depression is a Mechanism for Breaking Habits
type: "concept"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Long-Term Depression (LTD) is a neuroscientific principle that can be leveraged to break bad habits. It involves weakening neural pathways by intentionally dissociating a trigger from its associated habitual response. This follows the principle that "neurons that stop firing together, stop wiring together." This can be done by inserting a pause or a different action between the trigger and the unwanted habit.
---
