FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/A Next Action Must Be the Absolute Next Physical Visible Activity Required to Move a Situation Forward.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [action-definition, clarity, execution, gtd]
title: A Next Action Must Be the Absolute Next Physical Visible Activity Required to Move a Situation Forward
---
CONTENT_START:

## A Next Action Must Be the Absolute Next Physical Visible Activity Required to Move a Situation Forward

A "Next Action" in GTD is not a project label, a category, or a vague directive—it is the single, absolute next physical and visible activity that must happen to move a situation forward. "Set meeting" is not a next action; "Draft email to Bob proposing three meeting times" is. The physical, visible, and immediate character of the definition is what prevents the psychological gap between intention and action—and what eliminates the executive function load of re-deciding "where do I start?" at the moment of doing.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Logotherapy Posits the Search for Meaning as the Primary Human Motivation.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [logotherapy, meaning, philosophy, psychology]
title: Logotherapy Posits the Search for Meaning as the Primary Human Motivation
---
CONTENT_START:

## Logotherapy Posits the Search for Meaning as the Primary Human Motivation

Logotherapy—from the Greek _logos_ (meaning)—is Viktor Frankl's therapeutic approach centred on the premise that the primary human motivation is not pleasure (Freud) or power (Adler) but the search for meaning. When this search is frustrated, the result is an "existential vacuum": a state of boredom, apathy, and emptiness that manifests as psychological distress. Treatment therefore does not focus on symptom reduction but on purpose discovery.

---
