FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Committing Thought to Language Reveals Actual Versus Assumed Beliefs.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [cognitive-dissonance, logic, self-reflection, writing]
title: Committing Thought to Language Reveals Actual Versus Assumed Beliefs
---
CONTENT_START:

## Committing Thought to Language Reveals Actual Versus Assumed Beliefs

Converting amorphous, unexpressed thoughts into coherent written language forces them to engage with logical structure. This engagement exposes a person's actual beliefs versus the beliefs they assumed they held. Thoughts remain internally consistent only because they remain unexamined; language demands precision, and precision surfaces contradiction. The act of writing is therefore a mechanism for challenging and refining internal logical consistency.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/The Unit of Software Engineering Is Shifting from Code Lines to Intent Expressions.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [agents, automation, productivity, software-engineering]
title: The Unit of Software Engineering Is Shifting from Code Lines to Intent Expressions
---
CONTENT_START:

## The Unit of Software Engineering Is Shifting from Code Lines to Intent Expressions

The fundamental unit of software engineering is shifting from writing individual lines of syntax to expressing high-level intent to autonomous agent clusters. In this model, human engineering productivity is constrained by the precision and clarity of the intent expressed, not by typing speed or syntax knowledge. Engineering work is migrating up the abstraction stack: from implementation to orchestration.

---
