FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Mental Models as Evolutionary Filters.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-10-20T09:52:00Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:30+00:00
status: "null"
tags: ["efficiency", "evolution", "perception", "TheHuman/Cognition"]
title: Mental Models as Evolutionary Filters
type: "null"
updated: 
---
---

Links: [[Mental Models MOC]]
CONTENT_START:

Our brains construct mental models as a highly efficient evolutionary strategy to manage an overwhelming amount of sensory information. These models act as subconscious filters, allowing us to navigate the world, make predictions, and decide on actions without having to consciously process every detail.

From a survival perspective, this is critical. Having pre-built, simplified models allows for the rapid, almost instantaneous decision-making needed to avoid threats and seize opportunities. The trade-off for this speed and efficiency is that we don't experience the model itself; we experience the world _through_ the model.

---
