FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Number Theory (Definition).md
---
aliases: ["Number Theory"]
created: 2025-11-01T11:22:13Z
last_reviewed: "2025-11-01T00:00:00.000Z"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:30+00:00
status: "seedling"
tags: ["fields", "topic/maths"]
title: Number Theory (Definition)
type: "definition"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Definition: Number theory is the branch of mathematics devoted to studying the deeper properties and patterns within the natural numbers and integers, particularly focusing on questions about divisibility, prime numbers, and solutions to equations in integers.

Scope: While arithmetic deals with basic computations, number theory probes the hidden structures—such as the distribution of primes, Diophantine equations, and modular arithmetic—that reveal profound patterns in the number system.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Writing Reduces Cognitive Load by Chunking Information.md
---
aliases: ["Chunking Information Through Writing"]
created: 2025-07-18T00:00:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
see_also: []
status: ""
superseded_by: ""
supersedes: ""
tags: []
title: Writing Reduces Cognitive Load by Chunking Information
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

For Bessie, who finds it difficult to process large amounts of information, the act of writing is a powerful strategy for mitigating cognitive overload. The process of writing naturally forces the breakdown of complex material into smaller, more manageable chunks.

By focusing on capturing one key idea at a time, her working memory is protected from becoming overwhelmed. This chunking allows for more effective processing and comprehension, turning an intimidating volume of information into a sequence of achievable steps.

