FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Card's Atrocity Paradigm Identifies Evil via Three Necessary Co-conditions.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [atrocities, claudia-card, heuristic, moral-philosophy]
title: "Card's Atrocity Paradigm Identifies Evil via Three Necessary Co-conditions"
---
CONTENT_START:

## Card's Atrocity Paradigm Identifies Evil via Three Necessary Co-conditions

Claudia Card's "Atrocity Paradigm" identifies evil through three jointly necessary conditions: (1) culpable wrongdoing—the agent is responsible and could have acted otherwise; (2) reasonable foreseeability—the harm was predictable from the agent's position; (3) intolerable harm—the harm destroys the basic conditions for a decent or tolerable life. All three must be present. Satisfying one or two shifts the classification to ordinary wrongdoing, negligence, or tragedy.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Autopoiesis The Self-Referential System.md
---
aliases: ["Autopoiesis", "Self-Referential System"]
created: 2025-08-23T17:10:01Z
creation_date: "2025-08-23T00:00:00.000Z"
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:36+00:00
status: "null"
tags: ["biology", "systems-theory", "topic/philosophy"]
title: Autopoiesis The Self-Referential System
type: "null"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

Autopoiesis (from Greek _auto-_ 'self' and _poiesis_ 'creation, production') is a concept where a system continuously produces and maintains itself. Its primary organization is geared towards its own continuation.

A living cell is a key example. It constantly uses energy to rebuild its own structures and maintain its boundaries, separating itself from its environment. In this view, the "drive for survival" is not an abstract goal but the defining operational principle of the system. The system's purpose _is_ its own existence.

---
