FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Flexible Routines Require Contingency Planning.md
---
aliases: ["Build Flexibility into Routines"]
created: 2025-10-29T16:10:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:33+00:00
status: "seedling"
tags: ["adaptability", "contingency-planning", "resilience", "routine"]
title: Flexible Routines Require Contingency Planning
type: "strategy"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

A routine's effectiveness is determined not just by its structure, but by its flexibility. Life is unpredictable, and a rigid routine will shatter at the first disruption. Therefore, building in contingency plans is essential.

For any given routine, consider what might go wrong and create a "Plan B" or even "Plan C." This proactive planning reduces the stress and decision fatigue that arise when things don't go as expected. Furthermore, having pre-determined calming strategies (e.g., using noise-cancelling headphones, stepping away for a few minutes, using a sensory object) can help manage the emotional dysregulation that often accompanies an unexpected change of plans.
---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Finding Your Right Difficult A Strategy for ADHD Engagement.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-10-24T15:20:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:33+00:00
status: ""
tags: ["challenge", "DMN", "engagement", "personal-growth", "TheHuman/Health/ADHD", "TheHuman/Motivation"]
title: Finding Your Right Difficult A Strategy for ADHD Engagement
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

For individuals with Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD), a crucial strategy for maintaining consistent engagement and preventing the brain from getting stuck in negative rumination (the "Demon" of the [[The DMN as a Demon - Negative Rumination in ADHD|Default Mode Network]]) is to actively seek out and cultivate what is termed "Finding Your Right Difficult."

This concept emphasizes identifying challenges that are stimulating enough to engage one's creativity and strengths, providing a consistent source of intrinsic motivation. It's about finding tasks or activities that are neither too easy (leading to boredom and disengagement) nor too hard (leading to overwhelm and avoidance), but perfectly aligned with the ADHD brain's need for novelty, interest, and challenge.

---
