FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Concurrent Task Overload Creates Non-Linear Administrative Overhead That Destroys Focus.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T13:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T18:07:35+00:00
tags: [administrative-overhead, focus, productivity, workload-management]
title: Concurrent Task Overload Creates Non-Linear Administrative Overhead That Destroys Focus
---
CONTENT_START:

## Concurrent Task Overload Creates Non-Linear Administrative Overhead That Destroys Focus

Taking on too many concurrent tasks does not create a proportional increase in administrative overhead—it creates a non-linear flood. Each additional open commitment generates coordination, update, and context-switching overhead that compounds across all existing commitments. Beyond a threshold, this overhead consumes all remaining capacity for deep work, and no amount of efficiency optimisation can recover it. Workload limits are a prerequisite for focus, not a nice-to-have.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/The Aha Moment as a Dopamine Reward.md
---
aliases: []
created: 2025-08-27T19:16:54Z
last_reviewed: "null"
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:26+00:00
status: "null"
tags: ["TheHuman/Motivation", "TheHuman/Neuroscience", "TheHuman/Psychology", "topic/learning"]
title: The Aha Moment as a Dopamine Reward
type: "null"
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

The feeling of cleverness experienced when grasping a new concept is a biological reward mechanism. When a new piece of information is successfully connected to existing knowledge (an "Aha!" moment), the brain releases a small burst of dopamine.

Dopamine is a neurotransmitter associated with pleasure, motivation, and reward. This system evolved to encourage behaviours beneficial for survival, including understanding the world.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Use Intentional Friction to Curb Impulse Spending.md
---
aliases: ["Spending Friction"]
created: 2025-11-18T14:38:00Z
last_reviewed: ""
modified: 2026-02-01T15:08:24+00:00
status: ""
tags: ["budgeting", "finance", "TheHuman/Habits", "TheHuman/Psychology"]
title: Use Intentional Friction to Curb Impulse Spending
type: ""
updated: 
---
CONTENT_START:

One of the most effective ways to manage [[High-Frequency Micro-Spending Drains Resources|high-frequency impulse spending]] is to introduce intentional friction into the purchasing process. When spending is too easy, it often happens without conscious thought.

By setting firm, proactive budget limits for problematic categories (like coffee or dining out), you force a moment of consideration. When the category is empty, you must make a conscious choice to [[Roll With the Punches (YNAB Rule 3)|move money from another goal]], creating a tangible sense of the trade-off. This friction transforms a mindless habit into a deliberate decision.
---
