FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/Frankl and Camus Both Confront Absurdity But Diverge on Whether Meaning Is Discoverable.md
---
created: 2026-04-10T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-04-10T16:52:03+00:00
tags: [existentialism, meaning, philosophy, resilience]
title: Frankl and Camus Both Confront Absurdity But Diverge on Whether Meaning Is Discoverable
---
CONTENT_START:

## Frankl and Camus Both Confront Absurdity But Diverge on Whether Meaning Is Discoverable

Both Viktor Frankl and Albert Camus begin from the same premise: life carries no guaranteed, pre-given meaning in a universe that returns no answers to human demands. Their responses diverge fundamentally. Frankl holds that meaning is _discoverable_ within the absurd—detectable in the world, in relationships, and in one's stance toward suffering—and that hope grounded in this discovery is honest. Camus holds that hope is _philosophical suicide_—a leap of faith that evades the Absurd rather than confronting it—and that the only authentic response is revolt: living with full awareness of meaninglessness while refusing to despair of the present moment. Frankl's framework is therapeutic; Camus's is philosophical and rebellious.

---
FILE: /Volumes/DAL/Zettelkasten/LLMeon/30_Library/100_zettelkasten/SoT - The Emotional Bottleneck Hypothesis.md
---
aliases: ["ADHD Emotional Regulation", "Emotional Bottleneck"]
created: 2025-08-26T00:00:00+00:00
modified: 2026-02-09T13:53:50+00:00
tags: ["adhd-optimization", "emotional-regulation", "prodos"]
title: SoT - The Emotional Bottleneck Hypothesis
type: "SoT"
---
CONTENT_START:

## The Claim

The primary operational bottleneck in ADHD is not cognitive—it is emotional. Memory deficits and motivation gaps are manageable with external systems (Extended Mind, PINCH, checklists). What actually stops movement is emotional dysregulation: shame, self-doubt, the paralysis that comes from feeling "especially weak," and the Rejection Sensitivity that makes starting feel dangerous.

---
